  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe heatwave continues across the region. A few showers and\nstorms linger from near Omaha to Harlan early this afternoon. \nThese showers and storms are expected to continue drifting \ntowards the northeast over the next couple hours. Little \nrainfall is expected with these. Otherwise, hot and humid \nconditions remains the main weather story. Temperatures climb\nwell into the 90s across the area under mostly sunny skies.\nTemperatures may approach the century mark over far southeast\nNebraska and southwest Iowa. With dewpoints remaining in the\nupper 60s to low 70s, expect heat index values in the upper 90s\nto low 100s. Heat index values may reach up to 105 across\nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa where a Heat Advisory\nremains in effect. The advisory has been expanded to include\nportions of south-central Nebraska as the heat continues to\nbuild.\n\nTonight sees limited relief as temperatures will struggle to\nfall into the low to mid 70s. A nocturnal low-level jet is\nanticipated to develop overnight across much of the Plains. Our\narea will reside on the northern terminus of this low-level jet\nand this supports additional shower and storm development \nduring the early morning hours on Thursday. These showers and \nstorms should remain relatively weak as they drift northeast \ninto early afternoon. Once the limited rain clears, another hot \nand humid day awaits. Thursday afternoon temperatures remain \nlargely the same as Wednesday, though they increase slightly \nover northeast Nebraska. Once again, widespread 90s with heat \nindex values in the low 100s will continue to be a hazard. \nHeading into Friday, expect nearly a carbon copy to Thursday. \nWith this in mind, the Heat Advisory has been extended through \nat least Friday.\n\nThis weekend offers little relief. While the ridge begins to\nflatten slightly, temperatures on Saturday may only be a degree\nor two lower. Thankfully, temperatures begin to fall on Sunday\nand especially next week. At least lower 90s and even upper 80s\nreturn to the area. This remains well-above normal, but is\nwelcomed relief from the oppressive heat. Unfortunately,\novernight low temperatures do not see nearly as much of a\ndecrease and should remain largely in the 70s. During this\ntime, an active low-level jet and passing weather systems to our\nnorth maintain at least a low (10-30%) chance of showers and\nstorms most nights as we close out the forecast period.",
